Spike #12A (PR #361) PDF extraction discipline caught two additional
misattributions in the May-12 KY literature review beyond the
Gray-Kubizňák correction from earlier today:

1. **arXiv:2207.06435 → arXiv:2205.05064 for HKLS 2022.** The original
   lit review listed arXiv:2207.06435 alongside the correct
   arXiv:2205.05064 for Hadar-Kapec-Lupsasca-Strominger 2022 "Holography
   of the Photon Ring" (CQG 39:215001). The wrong ID actually resolves
   to Baiguera-Cederle-Penati "Supersymmetric Galilean Electrodynamics"
   — unrelated paper. The user-facing brief for Spike #12A inherited
   the wrong ID; the subagent's PDF-extraction caught it and verified
   arXiv:2205.05064 as the correct HKLS paper.

2. **arXiv:2309.02262 author attribution Xue-Jiang-Zhang 2023 (not
   Hadar-Lupsasca-Strominger).** Original lit review left the authors
   unstated; the user prompt for Spike #12A guessed "Hadar-Lupsasca-
   Strominger 2023" based on title similarity. Subagent's PDF
   verification: actual authors are Xue, Jiang, and Zhang. The
   paper title ("Notes on emergent conformal symmetry for black
   holes") was correct.

Both corrections applied to table rows 11 + 12 with explanatory
annotations capturing the verification chain.

DISCIPLINE PATTERN

Three misattribution corrections caught now via the same discipline:
  1. Gray-Kubizňák 2024 (caught by Spike #11)
  2. Hadar-Kapec-Lupsasca-Strominger 2022 arXiv ID (caught by Spike #12A)
  3. Xue-Jiang-Zhang 2023 (caught by Spike #12A)

In all three cases the literature-review subagent OR the user prompt
got the citation wrong; the math-spike subagent caught it by extracting
the actual paper PDF and verifying. The PDF-extraction-and-verify
discipline is becoming a load-bearing project standard for citation
hygiene. Worth memory-saving as a permanent practice.

Per the project's [[project_may8_commit_gemini_taint_caveat]]: citation
accuracy is a first-class concern; subagent verification catching
attribution errors is the gold standard.

